---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import editor from '../../assets/img/heygen-editor.png';
import editorBefore from '../../assets/img/heygen-editor-before.png';
import layout1 from '../../assets/img/heygen-layout-1.png';
import layout2 from '../../assets/img/heygen-layout-2.png';
import layout3 from '../../assets/img/heygen-layout-3.png';
import layout4 from '../../assets/img/heygen-layout-4.png';
import timelineBefore from '../../assets/img/heygen-timeline-before.png';
import timelineAfter from '../../assets/img/heygen-timeline-after.png';
import playbarFloating from '../../assets/img/heygen-playbar-floating.png';
import playbarNone from '../../assets/img/heygen-playbar-none.png';
import playbarRow from '../../assets/img/heygen-playbar-row.png';
import flows from '../../assets/img/heygen-flows.png';
import iosHow from '../../assets/img/heygen-ios-how.png';
import iosTopic from '../../assets/img/heygen-ios-topic.png';
import iosConsent from '../../assets/img/heygen-ios-consent.png';

// Every frame is a region of one of the Webflow case page's own images, cut so that the deck's
// annotations stay out of the picture: the image shows, the label names, the words argue. The
// before and the after share the first screen; the play bar's three states are the band between
// canvas and timeline, cut tight and stacked on the words' column after the words that set up
// the turn.
const eaton = {
  when: '2022.06 – 2022.08', org: 'Eaton Corporation', role: 'UX Design Intern', title: 'RapidLinkX',
  sub: 'An IoT configuration tool for airport conveyor systems, with parameter editing and error monitoring.',
  to: '/projects/eaton',
};
---
<Case
  title="Heygen — Kyle Xu"
  description="Product Design Intern at Heygen, June–October 2023. The AI video editor re-architected around the script with an updated component library — engagement +32.1%, task completion +24.3% against the flow it replaced — then a first iOS companion MVP for avatar creation, delivered in a three-week sprint."
  ogImage="/og-heygen.jpg"
  eyebrow="Heygen · Product Design Intern · June – October 2023"
  deck="One page had become the ceiling: customers were making multi-scene videos in an editor built for a single script. I moved the script into the sidebar and gave the timeline a ruler and a transport row; then a first iOS companion for recording an avatar, in three weeks."
  next={eaton}
>
  <Fragment slot="claim">The video editor, <span class="nb">re-architected</span> around the script.</Fragment>
  <Figure slot="hero" set="split" evidence="Shipped" loading="eager"
    items={[
      { src: editorBefore, name: 'Before', note: 'one page', alt: 'The old Heygen editor: a dark sidebar of avatar thumbnails, a canvas, and under it a panel with one script box, one voice card with a speed slider, and a timeline holding a single card' },
      { src: editor, name: 'After', note: 'what shipped', alt: 'The Heygen video editor as shipped: two script cards in a dark panel on the left, an avatar on a white canvas, and below them a transport row and a timeline with a ruler and playhead' },
    ]}
    caption="Left, what Heygen had: a script box under the canvas, one voice, a one-card timeline. Right, what shipped: the script in the sidebar, the timeline under its own transport row." />

  <section class="col sec" aria-label="In thirty seconds">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Scope</dt>
        <dd>The desktop editor — script panel, timeline, component library — as its only designer; then the iOS companion MVP for avatar creation, a <span class="nb">three-week</span> sprint.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Shipped</dt>
        <dd>The <span class="nb">re-architected</span> editor with its updated component library. The iOS MVP reached a <span class="nb">mid-fidelity</span> prototype and early validation, then was postponed.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Outcome</dt>
        <dd>
          <ul class="nums">
            <li><b>+32.1%</b><span>engagement</span></li>
            <li><b>+24.3%</b><span>task completion</span></li>
          </ul>
          <span class="line">Measured on the new editor against the <span class="nb">one-page</span> flow it replaced.</span>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-hard">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-hard">Why it was hard</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Heygen started with one job, an avatar reading a script, and the editor was built for it: the script box under the canvas, a row of scene cards for a timeline, all on one page with nothing to learn. By 2023 the customers were studios, creators and businesses making <span class="nb">multi-scene</span> videos, and the one page had become the ceiling: no room for a second script, no way to see time.</p>
      <p><span class="nb">Text-to-speech</span> was still the core of the product, so whatever came next had to give the script more room. Product and engineering read the scope differently and I was the only designer on it, so I proposed a <span class="nb">one-week</span> design sprint to get everyone to the same starting layout.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-script">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-script"><span class="n">1</span>Where the script lives</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The timeline needed the bottom of the page, which freed the script's old spot and raised the question of where it should go. I drew four layouts. Two added a <span class="nb">right-hand</span> panel, easy to extend, but it moved the script to the far edge of the screen and made it one panel among several. Two moved it into the left sidebar, as a tab beside Template, Avatar, Text, Element and Asset.</p>
      <p>I chose the plainest: the sidebar carries the script and nothing else moves. It was the least extensible option, and the sheet said so, but an existing user would open the new editor and still know where they were.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="four" evidence="Concept"
        items={[
          { src: layout1, note: 'A right panel for script and actions', alt: 'A block diagram: sidebar on the left, canvas in the middle, script and action panels stacked on the right, timeline along the bottom' },
          { src: layout2, note: 'Script right, actions over the canvas', alt: 'A block diagram: sidebar on the left, canvas with an action panel over its lower edge, a script panel on the right, timeline along the bottom' },
          { src: layout3, note: 'Script in the sidebar, actions below', alt: 'A block diagram: sidebar and script together on the left, canvas in the middle, an action panel between canvas and timeline' },
          { src: layout4, note: 'Sidebar, nothing else moves — chosen', alt: 'A block diagram: sidebar and script together on the left, canvas in the middle, timeline along the bottom, nothing else' },
        ]}
        caption="Four layouts from the sprint week, the script in orange." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-timeline">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-timeline"><span class="n">2</span>A timeline that shows time</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The old timeline showed cards, not time: no ruler, no playhead, and state changes so quiet that people weren't sure what they'd selected. A full <span class="nb">multi-track</span> suite would have been too much for the beginners who were most of the base.</p>
      <p>I kept the three tracks — avatar, script, audio — and gave them a ruler, a playhead, an expand toggle and clearer drag states. Each panel got a visible boundary, and whatever appears in two panels stays in sync, so a scene selected in the timeline lights up on the canvas. We tested it with agencies and individual creators, and what changed afterwards was mostly text: stronger state cues on the script cards, hotkey hints where a tooltip had been.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="stack" evidence="Shipped"
        items={[
          { src: timelineBefore, name: 'Before', alt: 'The old timeline: a play button, then three rows of cards for the avatar, the script and the scenes, with coarse marks at 0, 15, 30 and 45 seconds and no playhead' },
          { src: timelineAfter, name: 'After', alt: 'The new timeline: an expand toggle, transport buttons, a ruler marked every ten seconds, a playhead reading 00:38:88, and the avatar, scene and script tracks in a fixed order' },
        ]}
        caption="Cards with coarse marks and no playhead; then a ruler every ten seconds, the current time on the playhead, and the three tracks in a fixed order." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-playbar">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-playbar"><span class="n">3</span>The play bar, three times</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>In the first round I drew a play bar floating over the canvas. It didn't get through — too much on the canvas — so I went the other way and proposed dropping the play bar altogether: the timeline had a playhead now, and one control fewer would read as simpler.</p>
      <p>In testing, people kept reaching for it. People preview constantly in this tool, fixing a line and playing it back, and the timeline's small transport sat too far from where they were looking. Engineering had run into trouble of its own with that version. So I put the play bar back, as a slim row between canvas and timeline.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="stack" narrow evidence="Shipped"
        items={[
          { src: playbarFloating, name: 'Floating', note: 'round one, turned down', alt: 'The band between canvas and timeline, with a play bar floating over the lower edge of the canvas: play, a progress line, 00:00 of 00:30, zoom' },
          { src: playbarNone, name: 'None', note: 'round two, my proposal', alt: 'The same band with no play bar: the canvas ends, and the timeline begins with a lone play button beside its tracks' },
          { src: playbarRow, name: 'A row', note: 'what shipped', alt: 'The same band with a slim row between canvas and timeline: previous, play, next, and zoom controls' },
        ]}
        caption="The band between canvas and timeline, cut from the same editor in each state." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-shipped">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-shipped">How it shipped</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The editor went to engineering as two flow maps, Text to Script and Timeline Editor, with each edge case drawn as its own state — an empty script, a generation error, a voice that can't take a pitch setting, a script over the word limit. With a small team, the maps doubled as the review list; the updated component library went across with them.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure src={flows} evidence="Supporting state"
        alt="Two flow maps of small screens headed Text to Script and Timeline Editor, each task a lane, each branch ending in a labelled state"
        caption="Both maps as handed over, each branch ending in a drawn state." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-ios">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-ios">The iOS companion</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Avatar creation was <span class="nb">desktop-only</span>, and its weakest link was the webcam. A phone's camera is better, so in a <span class="nb">three-week</span> sprint at the end of the internship I led the first iOS MVP for recording an avatar: scope set with the CPO and product team, 10+ user interviews, six internal evaluation rounds, and a <span class="nb">mid-fidelity</span> prototype.</p>
      <p>The decision inside it was the recording screen: most people freeze without a script, so the teleprompter offers a topic and paces a <span class="nb">thirty-second</span> take, then a <span class="nb">two-minute</span> one. Consent is a spoken sentence recorded in the same session, so the footage carries its own authorisation.</p>
      <p>Early validation was positive. Against Heygen's other initiatives the MVP showed less growth potential, and it was indefinitely postponed. That was the right call. A prototype that works is not, by itself, a case for building the product.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="three" evidence="Prototype"
        items={[
          { src: iosConsent, name: 'Consent', note: 'read to camera', alt: 'A phone screen with the camera on the presenter and a consent script beneath: I, Kuanghong Xu, hereby declare that I authorize HeyGen to use the footage of me to build a HeyGen avatar' },
          { src: iosTopic, name: 'A topic', note: 'for a take without a script', alt: 'A phone screen asking you to choose a topic you feel comfortable talking about for thirty seconds: freestyle, tell us about yourself, your hometown, a product update, your business, a training lesson' },
          { src: iosHow, name: 'How it works', note: 'the first screen', alt: 'A phone screen headed How it works: footage plus consent, five minutes, then an instant avatar with your voice clone, and a Continue button' },
        ]}
        caption="Three screens from the mid-fidelity prototype." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-outcome">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-outcome">Outcome</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Measured against the <span class="nb">one-page</span> flow it replaced, the new editor lifted engagement 32.1% and task completion 24.3%. It shipped with its component library; the iOS MVP stayed a prototype.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="signoff">
    <p class="stance">The best decision in this project is the one I reversed.</p>
    <p>I proposed dropping the play bar; a usability test showed people reaching for it. It's the clearest record I have of a test changing my mind before engineering built on the wrong assumption.</p>
  </Fragment>
</Case>
